{
  "id": "murphy-shakti-60min-bilateral-hippocampus-fast",
  "name": "Shakti 60-min bilateral session: hippocampal signal (fast alternation)",
  "category": "spiritual",
  "evidence": "claim",
  "origin": "Todd Murphy (8 Coil Shakti)",
  "uT": 1,
  "summary": "Murphy's single 60-minute session 'to try to create an intense experience in ONE session': the hippocampal 'LTP' signal over both temporal lobes on the 8 Coil Shakti crosses, with the two coil pairs taking turns every half-second (the vendor's fast alternation).",
  "setting": "Blindfold, earplugs or a sound-blocking headset (the vendor: 'substituting white noise won't work'), no interruptions and no plans afterwards. Murphy: 'For the first twenty minutes, not much happens.'",
  "schedule": "A one-off for a single intense experience, not a series (the vendor: repeating it 'might eventually elicit an unpleasant experience'); at most once every 3 weeks.",
  "notes": "Four coils in a cross over each temporal lobe, as in the 8 Coil Shakti chart: top C5/C6, bottom T9/T10, front FT7/FT8, back TP7/TP8. One stereo channel drives the top and bottom coils of both crosses, the other the front and back coils; the vendor says single long sessions 'should be bilateral'. The vendor's optional fast mode switches channels 'twice each second' and, it says, 'emerged as the most effective' in the original developers' lab tests; here (two tracks, the signal gated in point tables) the top and bottom coils and the front and back coils take turns every 0.5 s. So that the half-second clock and the signal repeat together, each train comes every 4.5 s (latency 3.76 s instead of about 4 s), and the trains alternately start on the top/bottom and on the front/back pair. murphy-shakti-60min-bilateral-hippocampus-slow has the standard mode. A biphasic pulse is never split between the pairs. Coil polarity follows the vendor's chart, one + and one − coil in each pair: on the left cross the top (C5) and front (FT7) coils are − and the bottom (T9) and back (TP7) coils +; on the right cross the top (C6) and front (FT8) coils are + and the bottom (T10) and back (TP8) coils −. The − coils have negative weights, which reverse their current as if the coil were turned over; + is taken as this helmet's normal drive. The hippocampal 'LTP' file is proprietary; it is approximated by a point table in the vendor's standard scheme (3-ms points): four theta bursts, each of four biphasic pulses 9 ms apart (after the 4-pulse bursts of Larson & Lynch 1986), one burst every 186 ms (5.4 Hz, the spectral peak the vendor reports for its LTP signal). Anxiety during a hippocampal session is, for Murphy, a sign that it has run too long; stop then. The vendor reports headache or pressure in about 2% with hippocampal signals; if so, lower the intensity by one third. Independent double-blind tests of God-Helmet-type fields found no field effect (Granqvist 2005; Gendle & McGrath 2012). Not for people with epilepsy, seizure history or a psychiatric condition; stop on distress.",
  "targets": ["left-hippocampus-head", "left-hippocampus-body", "left-hippocampus-tail", "right-hippocampus-head", "right-hippocampus-body", "right-hippocampus-tail"],
